---
import DocsLayout from "../_components/DocsLayout.bangala"
import CodeBlock from "../_components/CodeBlock.bangala"

const quickstart = `npm create bangala@latest my-site
cd my-site
npm install
npm run dev`;

const firstPage = `---
const name = props.name ?? "Ada"
---
<h1>Hello {name}</h1>
<p>This page ships no framework JavaScript.</p>`;

const headings = [
  { id: "what-is-bangala", label: "What is Bangala?" },
  { id: "why-not-next", label: "Why not Next.js?" },
  { id: "mental-model", label: "Mental model" },
  { id: "start-here", label: "Start here" },
];
---
<DocsLayout
  title="Documentation | bangala.js"
  description="Documentation officielle de bangala.js : framework HTML-first, islands explicites, routing fichier, layouts, static generation et API."
  path="/docs"
  section="Documentation"
  lead="Bangala is an HTML-first framework for shipping sites that stay fast by default. Write .bangala files, prerender HTML, add islands only where the page truly needs JavaScript."
  headings={headings}
  next={{ href: "/docs/installation", label: "Installation" }}
>
  <h2 id="what-is-bangala">What is Bangala?</h2>
  <p>Bangala compiles <code>.bangala</code> files into ESM modules with an async <code>render(props)</code> function. The output is plain HTML. Client JavaScript appears only when a component is marked with <code>client:load</code>, <code>client:idle</code>, or <code>client:visible</code>.</p>

  <CodeBlock filename="Terminal" lang="bash" code={quickstart}/>

  <h2 id="why-not-next">Why not Next.js?</h2>
  <p>Next.js is excellent when you want the React platform. Bangala is for the other case: content-heavy sites, documentation, landing pages, blogs, and product pages where HTML should be the default and JavaScript should be an explicit decision.</p>
  <div class="docs-callout">
    <p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Bangala is not a Next.js clone. It competes by being smaller, more direct, and HTML-first. Routing, layouts, and static generation are familiar; the runtime model is intentionally lighter.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="mental-model">Mental model</h2>
  <div class="docs-grid">
    <a class="docs-card" href="/docs/syntax"><strong>1. Write HTML-first pages</strong><span>Use frontmatter, expressions, conditionals, loops, components, and one default slot.</span></a>
    <a class="docs-card" href="/docs/routing"><strong>2. Let files become routes</strong><span><code>pages/index.bangala</code>, <code>[slug]</code>, and <code>[...catchAll]</code> map to URLs.</span></a>
    <a class="docs-card" href="/docs/layouts"><strong>3. Compose with layouts</strong><span>Use <code>_layout.bangala</code> files to wrap route trees, outermost first.</span></a>
    <a class="docs-card" href="/docs/islands"><strong>4. Hydrate islands explicitly</strong><span>Only interactive components load client modules; static content stays static.</span></a>
  </div>

  <CodeBlock filename="pages/index.bangala" code={firstPage}/>

  <h2 id="start-here">Start here</h2>
  <p>New project? Start with <a href="/docs/installation">Installation</a>. Learning the file format? Read <a href="/docs/syntax">.bangala syntax</a>. Building a real site? Jump to <a href="/docs/examples">Examples</a>, <a href="/docs/layouts">Layouts</a>, and <a href="/docs/static-generation">Static Generation</a>.</p>
</DocsLayout>
